{
  "dom_id": "step-q351051",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{ft/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}-3,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{ft/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+\\int (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{t}-3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{6}{2}-3(2)+C \\\\ 1 & =-9+C \\\\ C & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-\\frac{6}{t}-3t+10.$",
